{"date": "2026-10-25", "post_id": 1503107, "title": "Dark Matter in a Simulated Universe", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-25-dark-matter-in-a-simulated-universe/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Is our universe haunted? It might look that way on this <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/dark-matter/\">dark matter</a> map. The gravity of unseen <a href=\"https://chandra.harvard.edu/xray_astro/dark_matter/\">dark matter</a> is the leading explanation for why <a href=\"https://ed.ted.com/lessons/dark-matter-how-does-it-explain-a-star-s-speed-don-lincoln\">galaxies rotate so fast</a>, why <a href=\"https://medium.com/starts-with-a-bang/galaxy-clusters-prove-dark-matters-existence-fd962c979458\">galaxies orbit clusters so fast</a>, why gravitational lenses so strongly deflect light, and how <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-may-12-illustris-simulation-of-the-universe/\">visible matter</a> is distributed. The featured image from the <a href=\"https://www.amnh.org/\">American Museum of Natural History</a>'s <a href=\"https://www.amnh.org/research/hayden-planetarium\">Hayden Planetarium</a> Space Show, Dark Universe, highlights one example of how pervasive dark matter might haunt our universe. In this frame from a <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zEEg1XkbXDo\">detailed computer simulation</a>, complex filaments of dark matter, shown in black, are strewn <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2011-october-3-dark-matter-movie-from-the-bolshoi-simulation/\">about the universe</a> like <a href=\"https://www.badspiderbites.com/giant-spider-web/\">spider webs</a>, while the relatively rare clumps of familiar <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baryon\">baryon</a>ic matter are colored orange. These simulations are good statistical matches to astronomical observations. In what is perhaps a <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/brown-scottish-fold-in-brown-thick-pile-blanket-NodtnCsLdTE\">scarier</a> turn of events, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/dark-matter/\">dark matter</a> -- although quite strange and in an <a href=\"https://home.cern/science/physics/dark-matter\">unknown form</a> -- is no longer thought to be the strangest source of <a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2008AmJPh..76..265N/abstract\">gravity in the universe</a>. That honor now falls to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/dark-energy/\">dark energy</a>, a more uniform source of <a href=\"http://www.preposterousuniverse.com/blog/2013/11/16/why-does-dark-energy-make-the-universe-accelerate/\">repulsive gravity</a> that seems to now dominate the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-may-31-supernova-1994d-and-the-unexpected-universe/\">expansion</a> of the entire <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/the-nature-of-the-universe-great-debate-in-1998-program/\">universe</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> Orion the Great", "credit": "<a href=\"https://kipac.stanford.edu/people/tom-abel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Tom Abel</a> &amp; <a href=\"https://www.slac.stanford.edu/~kaehler/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Ralf Kaehler</a> (<a href=\"https://kipac.stanford.edu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">KIPAC</a> , <a href=\"https://www6.slac.stanford.edu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">SLAC</a>), <a href=\"https://www.amnh.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">AMNH</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://kipac.stanford.edu/people/tom-abel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Tom Abel</a> &amp; <a href=\"https://www.slac.stanford.edu/~kaehler/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Ralf Kaehler</a> (<a href=\"https://kipac.stanford.edu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">KIPAC</a> , <a href=\"https://www6.slac.stanford.edu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">SLAC</a>), <a href=\"https://www.amnh.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">AMNH</a>", "alt": "The picture shows a computer simulation of the matter distribution in our universe with dark matter shown in a dark color on a light background. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-25-dark-matter-in-a-simulated-universe/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2021/october/DarkMatter_KipacAmnh_1200.jpg?w=1200&h=900&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 25 - Dark Matter in a Simulated Universe</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-25-dark-matter-in-a-simulated-universe/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Is our universe haunted? It might look that way on this dark matter map. The gravity of unseen dark matter is the leading explanation for why galaxies rotate so fast, why galaxies orbit clusters so fast, why gravitational lenses so strongly deflect light, and how visible matter is distributed. The featured image from the American Museum of Natural History&#039;s Hayden Planetarium Space Show, Dark Universe, highlights one example of how pervasive dark matter might haunt our universe. In this frame from a detailed computer simulation, complex filaments of dark matter, shown in black, are strewn about the universe like spider webs, while the relatively rare clumps of familiar baryonic matter are colored orange. These simulations are good statistical matches to astronomical observations. In what is perhaps a scarier turn of events, dark matter -- although quite strange and in an unknown form -- is no longer thought to be the strangest source of gravity in the universe. That honor now falls to dark energy, a more uniform source of repulsive gravity that seems to now dominate the expansion of the entire universe.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: Orion the Great\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"dark matter in a simulated universe\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Dark Matter in a Simulated Universe\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-25-dark-matter-in-a-simulated-universe/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2021/october/DarkMatter_KipacAmnh_1200.jpg?w=1200&#038;h=900&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2021/october/DarkMatter_KipacAmnh_1200.jpg?w=1200&#038;h=900&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"The picture shows a computer simulation of the matter distribution in our universe with dark matter shown in a dark color on a light background. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-25T04:19:48-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-25T04:19:53-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Dark Matter in a Simulated Universe\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2021/october/DarkMatter_KipacAmnh_1200.jpg?w=1200&#038;h=900&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 25<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2021/october/DarkMatter_KipacAmnh_1200.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/cds/apod/apod/2021/october/DarkMatter_KipacAmnh_1200.jpg\" alt=\"The picture shows a computer simulation of the matter distribution in our universe with dark matter shown in a dark color on a light background. Please see the explanation for more detailed information.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Dark Matter in a Simulated Universe </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://kipac.stanford.edu/people/tom-abel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Tom Abel</a> &amp; <a href=\"https://www.slac.stanford.edu/~kaehler/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Ralf Kaehler</a> (<a href=\"https://kipac.stanford.edu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">KIPAC</a> , <a href=\"https://www6.slac.stanford.edu/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">SLAC</a>), <a href=\"https://www.amnh.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">AMNH</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Is our universe haunted? It might look that way on this <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/dark-matter/\">dark matter</a> map. The gravity of unseen <a href=\"https://chandra.harvard.edu/xray_astro/dark_matter/\">dark matter</a> is the leading explanation for why <a href=\"https://ed.ted.com/lessons/dark-matter-how-does-it-explain-a-star-s-speed-don-lincoln\">galaxies rotate so fast</a>, why <a href=\"https://medium.com/starts-with-a-bang/galaxy-clusters-prove-dark-matters-existence-fd962c979458\">galaxies orbit clusters so fast</a>, why gravitational lenses so strongly deflect light, and how <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2014-may-12-illustris-simulation-of-the-universe/\">visible matter</a> is distributed. The featured image from the <a href=\"https://www.amnh.org/\">American Museum of Natural History</a>'s <a href=\"https://www.amnh.org/research/hayden-planetarium\">Hayden Planetarium</a> Space Show, Dark Universe, highlights one example of how pervasive dark matter might haunt our universe. In this frame from a <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zEEg1XkbXDo\">detailed computer simulation</a>, complex filaments of dark matter, shown in black, are strewn <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2011-october-3-dark-matter-movie-from-the-bolshoi-simulation/\">about the universe</a> like <a href=\"https://www.badspiderbites.com/giant-spider-web/\">spider webs</a>, while the relatively rare clumps of familiar <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baryon\">baryon</a>ic matter are colored orange. These simulations are good statistical matches to astronomical observations. In what is perhaps a <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/brown-scottish-fold-in-brown-thick-pile-blanket-NodtnCsLdTE\">scarier</a> turn of events, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/dark-matter/\">dark matter</a> -- although quite strange and in an <a href=\"https://home.cern/science/physics/dark-matter\">unknown form</a> -- is no longer thought to be the strangest source of <a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2008AmJPh..76..265N/abstract\">gravity in the universe</a>. That honor now falls to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/dark-energy/\">dark energy</a>, a more uniform source of <a href=\"http://www.preposterousuniverse.com/blog/2013/11/16/why-does-dark-energy-make-the-universe-accelerate/\">repulsive gravity</a> that seems to now dominate the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2015-may-31-supernova-1994d-and-the-unexpected-universe/\">expansion</a> of the entire <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/the-nature-of-the-universe-great-debate-in-1998-program/\">universe</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> Orion the Great\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-24-rosettas-selfie/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261025/html"}